rict fundamentals with incumbent Crane running", "Most raters call it Likely R, though two say Lean R", "Conflicting polls: Democratic-sponsored GBAO shows a 2-point race, Republican-sponsored shows an 11-point lead", "Markets price Crane around 67-69%, and he holds a large cash advantage"], "rationale": "The district's R+7 lean, Crane's incumbency and fundraising edge, and a Likely R rating consensus point to a clear but not safe Republican advantage. A recent Democratic-sponsored poll showing a 2-point race and markets at roughly a one-in-three Democratic chance justify a narrower margin than the rating consensus implies.", "watch": "Any additional independent poll confirming the narrow GBAO margin, or a strong Democratic fundraising and outside-spending surge, would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Eli Crane (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Eli Crane defends a northeastern Arizona seat that leans Republican by about seven points, in a rematch against former Navajo Nation President Jonathan Nez. Crane has raised roughly $9.5 million and holds a cash advantage, while Nez has raised about $3.5 million with little outside help so far. Most expert raters call the race Likely Republican, though two rate it Lean Republican, and prediction markets give Crane roughly a two-thirds chance.\n\nPolling is sparse and partisan-tinged: a Democratic-sponsored August survey showed Nez within two points, while a Republican-sponsored poll showed Crane up eleven. Headlines highlight Nez campaigning on water and cost-of-living issues and hoping for buyer's remorse against Crane. The forecast expects Crane to win by about five points, with meaningful uncertainty given the thin and conflicting polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1703, "margin": -5.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2296, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2331, "margin": -5.0}]}